    "body": "@annevk,  by local resource, I mean a string or file on the local machine. Something where you would use this approach - `var worker = new Worker(URL.createObjectURL(blob));`. In this case the worker has no domain or complete location.  Actually, a better title might have been \"Set Location\".\r\n\r\nHere's a use case example. I'm creating a worker that opens a WebSocket when the page loads (tied to the page location/url), where the worker code is sent in the page. I'm also using classes and modules, with things like\r\n```\r\n   // builder.js\r\n   export class SocketBuilder{ }\r\n\r\n    // example stuff in the Worker construction file (workerSource.js) /source\r\n    import { SocketBuilder } from \"/sockets/builder.js\"\r\n    const protocol = self.location.protocol === \"https:\" ? \"wss\" : \"ws\";\r\n    const port = self.location.port ? `${self.location.port}` : \"\";\r\n```\r\nWhen I create the worker from a file on the server `new Worker(\"/workerSource.js\", { type:\"module\" })` , the import and location pieces work fine.\r\n\r\nBut when I do this from \"local\" data there are difficulties.\r\n```\r\n// In html page\r\n<script type=\"module\">\r\n    var response = \"self.onmessage=function(e){import {SocketBuilder} from '/sockets/builder.js'; console.log(location); postMessage('Worker: '+e.data);}\";\r\n    var blob = new Blob([response], { type: 'application/javascript' });\r\n    var worker = new Worker(URL.createObjectURL(blob), { type:\"module\" });\r\n</script>\r\n```\r\nHere, the host, protocol, etc. are empty. Also, (don't know if this is a bug) `import` throws an unexpected token error on `{`.\r\n\r\nThis idea allows `new Worker()` to accept a url, that sets the `WorkerLocation`. \r\n",
